== 3. Of Married Folks

A family is divided into various combinations, or couples, of persons.

A couple is that by which two persons, standing in mutual relation to each other, are joined together, as it were, into one. And of these two, the one is always higher and bears rule; the other is lower and yields subjection.

Couples are of two sorts: principal, or less principal.

The principal is the couple of married folks; and they are so called with respect to marriage.

Marriage is the lawful joining of the two married persons, that is, of one man and one woman, into one flesh. Such was the first institution of marriage (Gen 2:21). It is expounded by our Savior Christ, Matt 19:6: “So they are no longer two, but one flesh.” And also by Paul, Eph 5:31: “For this reason a man will leave his father and mother and be joined#footnote[προσκολληθήσεται] to his wife” (as two boards are joined together with glue) “and the two will become one flesh.” Therefore this is an eternal law of marriage, that two, and not three or four, shall be one flesh. And for this reason the fathers of old who had many wives and concubines, though it may be that through custom they sinned in ignorance, are yet in no way to be excused.

Marriage in itself is a thing indifferent (neither commanded nor forbidden), and the kingdom of God consists no more in it than in food and drink. And yet it is a state in itself far more excellent than the condition of single life. For first, it was ordained by God in Paradise, above and before all other states of life, in Adam’s innocence before the fall. Again, it was instituted on a most serious and solemn consultation among the three persons of the holy Trinity. Gen 1:26: “Let Us make man in Our image, after Our likeness, to rule over …” Gen 2:18: “Jehovah Elohim also said, ‘It is not good for the man to be alone. I will make for him a suitable helper.’” Thirdly, the manner of this joining was excellent, for God joined our first parents Adam and Eve together directly. Fourthly, God gave a large blessing to the state of marriage, saying, “Be fruitful and multiply, and fill the earth.” Lastly, marriage was made and appointed by God himself to be the fountain and seedbed of all other sorts and kinds of life, in the commonwealth and in the church.

Now if mankind had continued in the uprightness and integrity it had by creation, the state of single life would have had no value or esteem among men; nor could it have had any place in the world without great contempt of God’s ordinance and blessing.

Nevertheless, since the fall, single life is in many respects far better than marriage for some men who have the gift of continency; yet not simply, but only by accident, in regard to the various calamities that came into the world by sin. For first, it frees a man from many great cares of household affairs. Again, it makes him much more fit and disposed to meditate on heavenly things without distraction of mind. Besides that, when dangers in matters belonging to this life are either present or impending, the single person is happy in this case, because he and his are more secure and safe than others who are in the married state. 1 Cor 7:8: “Now to the unmarried and widows I say this: It is good for them to remain unmarried, as I am.” v. 26: “Because of the present crisis, I think it is good for a man to remain as he is.” v. 28: “But if you do marry, you have not sinned. And if a virgin marries, she has not sinned. But those who marry will face troubles in this life.” v. 32: “I want you#super[+] to be free from concern.”

The end of marriage is fourfold.

The first is the procreation of children, for the propagation and continuance of the seed and posterity of man on the earth. Gen 1:28: “Be fruitful and multiply, and fill the earth and subdue it.” Gen 9:1. 1 Tim 5:14.

The second is the procreation of a holy seed, so that the church of God may be kept holy and chaste, and there may always be a holy company of men to worship and serve God in the church from age to age. Mal 2:15: “Has not the #smallcaps[Lord] made them one, having a portion of the Spirit? And why one? Because He seeks godly offspring.”

The third is that, after the fall of mankind, it might be a sovereign means to avoid fornication, and so to subdue and quench the burning lusts of the flesh. 1 Cor 7:2: “But to avoid sexual immorality, each man should have his own wife, and each woman her own husband.” v. 9: “But if they cannot control themselves, let them marry. For it is better to marry than to burn with passion.” For this reason some schoolmen err, who hold that the private coming together of husband and wife cannot be without sin unless it is done for the procreation of children. Lombard, the Master of the Sentences, says the contrary, namely, “that marriage before the fall was only a duty, but now since the fall it is also a remedy.”

#emph[The fourth end is that the married parties may by it perform the duties of their callings in a better and more helpful way.] Prov 31:11: “The heart of her husband trusts in her, and he lacks nothing of value.” v. 13: “She selects wool and flax and works with eager hands.”

Marriage is free to all orders and sorts of men without exception, even to those who have the gift of continency; but for those who cannot abstain, it is, by the express commandment of God, necessary. Heb 13:4: “Marriage should be honored by all and the marriage bed kept undefiled.” 1 Cor 7:9: “But if they cannot control themselves, let them marry.”

From this it appears to be a clear case that the commandment of the Pope of Rome, by which he forbids marriage to certain persons, namely the clergy, is purely diabolical. For so the apostle writes, 1 Tim 4:1: “Now the Spirit expressly states that in later times some will abandon the faith to follow deceitful spirits and the teachings of demons,” v. 3: “They will prohibit marriage.”

But various things are alleged against this doctrine.

#emph[Objection 1.] The apostle commands the married among the Corinthians to abstain by consent for a time, “so you#super[+] may devote yourselves to fasting and prayer” (1 Cor 7:5). I answer that Paul does not speak in that place of the daily and common service of God in prayer, but of the solemn and extraordinary exercise of it, which was joined with fasting and abstinence in common dangers and calamities.

#emph[Objection 2.] 1 Cor 7:32: “The unmarried man is concerned about the work of the Lord, how he can please the Lord. But the married man is concerned about the affairs of this world.” #emph[Answer.] The place is not to be understood universally, of all, but indefinitely, that is, of some: of those married persons who are carnal and fleshly. And he who is married is to be so concerned for the things of the world that he ought to have, and may have as well, a special regard for the things that concern God and his kingdom.

#emph[Objection 3.] 1 Tim 5:11: “The younger widows,” says the apostle, “will want to marry, and thus will incur judgment because they are setting aside their first faith.” #emph[Answer.] By “the first faith” Paul means the promise and vow they made to Christ when they were baptized. And they are said there to “grow wanton against Christ” by denying or renouncing that faith, and not otherwise.

#emph[Objection 4.] If a man earnestly and from his heart asks for the gift of continency, God will give it to him; and therefore he need not marry.

#emph[Answer.] The gifts of God are of two sorts: some are general, some are particular. General gifts are those that God gives generally to all, and these may be obtained if they are asked lawfully, according to God’s word. Jas 1:5: “Now if any of you#super[+] lacks wisdom, he should ask God, who gives generously to all without finding fault, and it will be given to him.” v. 6: “But he must ask in faith, without doubting.” Particular gifts are those given only to certain men. Of this sort is the gift of continency and the like, which, though they are often and earnestly asked for, are yet seldom or never granted to some men. This appears in Paul’s example, who, being buffeted by Satan, says of himself, “Three times I pleaded with the Lord to take it away from me. But He said to me, ‘My grace is sufficient for you’” (2 Cor 12:8, 9).
